\section{Dependence as a Region Interface}

Weise, Crew, Ernst, and Steensgaard's \textit{Value Dependence Graphs: Representation without Taxation} replaces extraneous sequencing in a CFG by dependence on computed values. Stanier and Watson's survey notes a difficulty: a demand-based representation can discard an undemanded computation that used to prevent the program from terminating. State dependence accounts for essential order not otherwise visible through result values. Reissmann, Meyer, Bahmann, and Sj\"alander's \textit{RVSDG: An Intermediate Representation for Optimizing Compilers} adds a further structural discipline: primitive computations and compound nodes inhabit nested regions with explicit interfaces.

Inside a region, each operation's input is wired to exactly one origin of the matching type, either a region argument or the output of another node in that region. Region results obey the same rule. A compound node maps its inputs and outputs to the arguments and results of its subregions; an arbitrary edge does not pierce a region boundary. Edges can stand for data or state, and the graph of dependencies within each region is acyclic. This is stronger than putting cycles for loops into a flat graph: repetition is part of a node's meaning, not part of the ordinary graph evaluation rule.

\section{Worked Example: A Zero-Trip Loop}

A \(\gamma\)-node chooses and evaluates one of several regions with matching signatures. A \(\theta\)-node evaluates its body at least once. The body yields a continuation predicate and a tuple that either feeds the next iteration or supplies the final outputs. To append \(0,1,\ldots,n-1\) to an external log when \(n>0\), combine both constructs. Let \(S\) be the log state, assume \(n\) fits the chosen integer type, and let \texttt{emit} consume and produce log state.

\begin{verbatim}
gamma(n > 0; n, S):
  false(n, S): yield S
  true(n, S):
    (i_out, bound_out, S_out) = theta(0, n, S):
      body(i, bound, s):
        s1 = emit(s, i)
        j  = i + 1
        yield (j < bound; j, bound, s1)
    yield S_out
\end{verbatim}

Initially the carried index is zero and the incoming log has no appended entries from this loop. At the beginning of iteration \(i\), the carried state has appended exactly the prefix of length \(i\). The body emits \(i\), then tests \(j=i+1\); the invariant proves by induction that the true region returns precisely the log with \(n\) appended entries. The false region forwards the original state. Dropping the \(\gamma\)-guard would emit zero even for \(n=0\). Dropping the state output of the body would lose the very dependence through which the externally visible result is demanded.

\section{Moving Computation and Reconstructing Control}

Boundary ports make some locality tests simple. A value that passes unchanged from argument to result of a \(\theta\)-body is loop invariant, as illustrated by \texttt{bound}. A pure, total calculation using invariant inputs can be considered for hoisting. Moving it outside the surrounding \(\gamma\) also crosses a possible zero-trip path. For computations with observable behavior, the guarantee of at least one iteration in a \(\theta\) does not justify arbitrary hoisting: effects preceding the operation, exceptional exits, repeated effects, and dependencies on the carried state still matter. For similar reasons, discarding an unused result is legitimate only if the graph retains whatever dependence is necessary to preserve traps, effects, and divergence under the IR's observation rules.

The RVSDG paper describes construction through control restructuring and demand annotation, then recovers a CFG at the other end. In the example the \(\gamma\)-node yields a branch and merge, and the \(\theta\)-node yields a loop header whose incoming arguments have entry and back-edge values. The internal region interface gives the compiler enough information to create those arguments; it does not prescribe the labels or the original CFG shape. An inexpensive structural verifier can check region-local wiring, acyclicity, and agreement of the loop's carried types. A semantic check of destruction can compare execution on zero, one, and many iterations, including the state passed across the back edge and at the final merge. Valid structure alone would not have caught replacing the false region by the loop body.

\section{The Same Port Discipline Across Procedures}

Reissmann and colleagues also use \(\lambda\)-nodes for function bodies, with separate ports for context dependencies and ordinary call arguments. The body is evaluated by an application, not merely by constructing the function's graph. For mutually recursive functions a \(\phi\)-node exposes the definitions to external callers and supplies internal recursion references as region arguments. This \(\phi\) is a recursive environment, not the predecessor-selecting \(\phi\) of SSA. In both this case and the loop example, an apparent control cycle is expressed by the semantics of a compound node; the local graph can still be traversed and checked as a DAG.
